  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period of \nbreezy to strong NE winds through early this week with \nstrongest winds along the coast, especially south of Cape \nHatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, combined with \nupcoming King Tide cycle lead to additional minor coastal \nimpacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore continue in the 7-9 foot\nrange and will remain in this range today, and then slowly \nsubside through the coming week, but long period swells will\ncontinue to cause issues for vulnerable areas in conjunction\nwith the building King Tides. For ocean-side: high tide is \nbetween 0700-0800edt SUN. Soundside high tides lag from 2 to 3 \nhours behind the oceanside tides. The National Parks Service and\nNCDOT have reported substantial beach erosion from south Nags \nHead to Buxton, along with significant overwash occurring on \nside streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous road conditions and \nflooding, NC-12 remains closed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to \nRodanthe. \n\nCoastal flooding will be likely well into this week. Even with \nwinds diminishing by mid week, \"sunny day\" coastal flooding \nacross much of the tidal regions of ENC around times of high \ntide will continue through at least mid week, then slowly\nimprove as the King Tides ease later in the week. The areas \nmost susceptible to street flooding will be low lying areas \nalong the Neuse and Bay rivers as well as Downeast Carteret \nCounty to North Topsail Beach which has already been seen during\nthe last two morning's high tides. \n\nThere is still potential that the headlines for TUE and WED may\nneed to be upgraded to coastal flood warnings. Will wait for \nthis decision after we see what SUN and MON bring. In addition, \nif seas increase any further than currently forecast, a period \nof coastal flood warnings may be needed for parts of the OBX, \nand will need to monitor trends.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pockets of moisture continue to be squeezed \nbetween the inland high and offshore low. Though almost all \nguidance keeps the bulk of precip activity off the coast and \nperhaps skirting OBX, some light returns continue on KMHX radar,\nbut most of this is in the form of insignificant non-\naccumulating sprinkles or drizzle. Most areas remain dry today,\nalong with seasonably cool temps in the low 70s interior to 60s\ncoast. Pleasantly cooler and drier air mass will remain with us\nthrough early this week, with near climo highs, to slightly \nbelow avg lows, esp Sun and Mon nights when good radiational \ncooling yet again sets up with lows well down into the 40s with \nsome 30s again possible. Hts/thicknesses then begin to build by\nmid week, with temps climbing back above climo with highs in the\nmid 70s, then upper 70s by weeks end. Lows will remain closer to\nclimo as dry airmass remains with a large diurnal temp range.",
